(D2 — Discrepancy 2 repro — RESOLVED, manual corrected. Old manual line
 2381-2383 (pre-0.4.9) said a `For each` over a list of maps "types the
 loop variable as a map". It does not: the loop variable carries the map
 TAG (so `is a map` fires on it — LST-55) but has no static type, and map
 access is a static check, so `entry's "tag"` inside the loop is a
 compile error. Extracting the element into a declared `map` variable
 first works (LST-57). LANGUAGE.md:2561-2575 now states the corrected
 rule verbatim and shows the position-loop idiom as the worked example.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D2.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   error: Map access target must be a map: entry
   (compile error - no binary is produced)
)
a list called holder is [{"tag": 1}, {"tag": 2}].
For each entry in holder, print entry's "tag".
